WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.770
A Charlie Brown Christmas has become a bonafide
holiday staple.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.670
If you’re an adult who’s watched the show
multiple times, there are several details

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:13.420
you may have noticed about this seasonal classic
that younger viewers likely miss.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.370
Let's investigate.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:20.280
Just as Pig-Pen is perpetually surrounded
by dust clouds and Schroeder is almost always

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.260
next to his piano, Linus is hardly ever seen
without his trusty blue blanket.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.500
He's got it with him outside in the snow,
and inside during rehearsals for the Christmas

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.500
play.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.440
"Linus, you've got to get rid of that stupid
blanket!"

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.840
He's quite clearly attached to the thing.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.280
Perhaps a little too attached.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.460
"You think you're so smart with that blanket.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.520
What are going to do with it when you grow
up?"

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.720
"Maybe I'll make it into a sportcoat."

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.969
You can't blame Linus for not wanting to let
the blanket go: It appears to be made of 100%

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.390
pure magic.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.950
It gets dragged through the snow all day without
getting soggy, and even serves as a remarkably

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.079
accurate slingshot.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:04.879
Wait, did he just ice-skate with his security
blanket in his hand?

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.579
And now this is happening.

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:11.030
Later on, Lucy tries to get him to put down
the blanket so that he can dress up like a

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.020
shepherd for the Christmas play.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.310
That's when Linus reveals that the blanket
can shapeshift, forming a perfect headdress

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.400
complete with a band in the middle.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.299
Who manufactures this Swiss Army Security
Blanket?

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:24.850
We simply must have one.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:29.219
A good chunk of A Charlie Brown Christmas
takes place at the local auditorium, where

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:32.459
the Peanuts gang is getting ready to put on
a Christmas play.

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:37.310
Charlie Brown is roped into the production
after he tells Lucy about his holiday depression,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.729
and she feels the play will help him get into
the holiday spirit.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:41.729
"Me?

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:44.409
You want me to be the director of the Christmas
play?"

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:46.560
"Sure, Charlie Brown.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.939
We need a director.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:49.369
You need involvement."

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.179
Charlie Brown eagerly leaps into his role
as director, but he can't seem to get anyone

00:01:54.179 --> 00:01:55.569
to listen to him.

00:01:55.569 --> 00:02:00.560
Perhaps more importantly, no one seems entirely
clear on what play they're even putting on.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.109
At one point, Lucy hands out scripts to various
kids for roles like "innkeeper" and "shepherd."

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:11.470
Obviously, this suggests a Nativity play ... but
then she also asks Snoopy:

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.470
"Can you be a sheep?

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.660
How 'bout a cow?

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.690
How 'bout a penguin?"

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:21.690
Quite the menagerie, all told.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.000
To complicated matters further, Schroeder
keeps trying to score the play with upbeat

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.270
jazz, and at one point, Lucy suggests herself
for a plum-sounding role:

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.000
"What about the Christmas queen?

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Hmm?"

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.390
So, is this some sort of highly conceptual,
staunchly avant-garde Nativity play?

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:39.910
Is it a variety show?

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.340
Does anyone really know?

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.990
To our ears, it all sounds a tad bit unfocused.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:50.240
A Charlie Brown Christmas begins by focusing
on the titular character's own lack of satisfaction

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.660
with the holiday.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.800
He tells Linus, in no uncertain terms:

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.760
"Christmas is coming, but I'm not happy.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.250
I don't feel the way I'm supposed to feel."

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:03.510
He also repeatedly complains that the holiday
has grown too commercial.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.830
His Scrooginess reaches dizzying new heights
when he sees Snoopy putting up a bunch of

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.270
decorations on his doghouse.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:14.400
That's when he realizes the beagle is decking
the halls for a Christmas light contest that's

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.530
promising a cash prize.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.680
"Oh no, my own dog gone commercial...

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.920
I can't stand it."

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.510
By the end of the special, we learn that Snoopy
not only entered the contest, but managed

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.230
to win first place.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:36.050
We admit that Snoopy's decorated doghouse
looks very charming… but we have a few concerns.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:40.850
He puts lights up all around the roof, hangs
a theoretically festive chain or two, and

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.230
even tops it all off with a star.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.490
There's nothing wrong with his decorations
per se, but it ultimately amounts to little

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.890
more than a strand of lights and a couple
of baubles hanging around a doghouse.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:55.950
How did this doghouse win out over all those
suburban properties?

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:00.170
Come to think of it, Charlie Brown's neighbors
don't appear to have put in much of an effort.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.260
Where's everyone's Christmas lights?

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.080
Apart from the ultra-commercial Christmas
tree lot, it looks like people just weren't

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.780
feeling it that year.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.590
Maybe Charlie Brown had every reason to be
depressed.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:16.290
When the Christmas play rehearsals don't seem
to be working, Charlie Brown makes a passionate

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.290
plea:

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.959
"Look, Charlie Brown — what do you want?"

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:19.959
"The proper mood.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:21.949
We need a Christmas tree!"

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:26.919
Lucy agrees and advises Charlie Brown to get
a great big aluminum Christmas tree.

00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:31.259
Charlie Brown and Linus subsequently head
to the tree lot, where they ultimately decide

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:35.770
to take the scrawny real tree that remains
one of the most famous parts of the show.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:40.020
The whole point of the scene is that Charlie
Brown picks this little twig of a tree, which

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.280
mirrors his own feelings about the season
… but when you look around at the rest of

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.680
the lot, you may have some questions.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.900
It's arranged like a traditional Christmas
tree lot, but it soon becomes clear that the

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.780
tiny tree is the only real tree there.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.259
The rest are made of aluminum.

00:04:56.259 --> 00:05:02.059
"This really brings Christmas close to a person."

00:05:02.059 --> 00:05:03.059
"Fantastic."

00:05:03.059 --> 00:05:07.539
Setting aside the lack of real trees for a
moment, let's ask the obvious question: Who's

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:12.029
selling aluminum Christmas trees outside where
it's clearly been snowing?

00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:14.889
Snow is wet, and the trees are made of delicate
metal.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:18.300
Surely that would have messed up the merchandise,
no?

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.580
When Charlie Brown brings the tiny tree back
from the lot and presents it to his friends,

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.409
they call him names and full-on laugh at him.

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:30.379
Then Linus makes a stirring speech, passionately
quoting scripture in the hopes of emphasizing

00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:32.380
the true meaning of Christmas.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:35.319
That's when Charlie Brown decides to take
the tree home.

00:05:35.319 --> 00:05:39.259
He thinks he can convince everyone that they
were wrong to make fun of the tree so long

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:41.289
as he can get the decorations right.

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:45.449
Charlie Brown gives up after the tree nearly
breaks in half from the weight of just one

00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:48.360
ornament, but then the other kids come along
to help.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:53.050
Linus wraps his blanket around the base, and
the kids use all the decorations from Snoopy's

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.569
doghouse to fill out the rest of the tree.

00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:59.569
By the time the kids are done decorating with
all the lights and baubles, the tree looks

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:03.889
like it's grown about a dozen extra branches
and a whole bunch of extra needles.

00:06:03.889 --> 00:06:07.180
In fact, it looks like a completely different
tree.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.039
Is it a Christmastime miracle?

00:06:09.039 --> 00:06:11.800
"What's going on here?"

